<Aside>
<strong>The log-partition function does triple duty.</strong> Inside the exponential
family $p(x\mid\eta) = h(x)\exp(\eta^\top T(x) - A(\eta))$, the normalizer
$A(\eta) = \log\int h(x)\exp(\eta^\top T(x))\,dx$, the <em>log-partition function</em>,
is a single object that encodes the whole geometry of the family in natural
coordinates:
$$
\nabla A(\eta) = \mathbb&#123;E&#125;_\eta[T(X)], \qquad
\nabla^2 A(\eta) = \mathrm&#123;Cov&#125;_\eta(T(X)) = I(\eta).
$$
$A$ is convex (its Hessian is a covariance matrix, hence positive semidefinite). Its
first derivative gives the mean of the sufficient statistic; its second derivative
gives the Fisher information in natural coordinates. The Legendre dual of $A$ is
(negative) <a href="/math/entropy-mutual-information/">entropy</a>, which is the
foundation of <a href="/math/variational-inference/">variational inference</a>.
</Aside>

<Figure title={String.raw`Figure 3e · Log-partition $A(\eta)$: slope = mean, curvature = Fisher info`} canvasId="fig3-logpartition" width="760" height="380" readoutId="fig3-logpartition-readout">
<Fragment slot="legend">
